    "body": "How do you monitor a large fleet of payment terminals without spending tens of thousands of dollars a month on geolocation API calls? That was the problem I was trying to solve but never got to finish. Rather than let the idea disappear, I turned it into an open-source proof-of-concept.\nThe core insight: most terminals don't move. A merchant's EFTPOS terminal sits on the same counter for months. If you resolve its location once and cache it against the cell towers it sees, you almost never need to call Google again. My Fermi estimate put annual external API costs in low four figures once the cache was warm — down from potentially hundreds of thousands of dollars at scale.\nThe PoC demonstrates the full architecture locally:\n→ PostGIS + GeoServer for spatial storage and live map layers\n→ Kotlin/Spring Boot processing service with cache-first location resolution\n→ OpenCellID cell tower data"
